WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.750
- It takes such a
desperate, obsessive focus

00:00:04.676 --> 00:00:08.426
to excel on a level that
I want to make movies.

00:00:09.434 --> 00:00:12.714
You have to be able to
laugh at everything.

00:00:12.714 --> 00:00:16.572
He who says he can and
he who says he can't

00:00:16.572 --> 00:00:18.844
are both usually right.

00:00:18.844 --> 00:00:22.594
- He's an American actor,
producer, rapper, and songwriter.

00:00:22.594 --> 00:00:24.184
He's been ranked as the most bankable

00:00:24.184 --> 00:00:26.427
movie star worldwide by Forbes.

00:00:26.427 --> 00:00:29.271
As of 2016, his films have grossed

00:00:29.271 --> 00:00:32.192
over $7.5 billion worldwide.

00:00:32.192 --> 00:00:33.932
He's Will Smith and here's my take

00:00:33.932 --> 00:00:36.487
on his Top 10 Rules for
Success, volume two.

00:00:36.487 --> 00:00:38.182
Rule number one is my personal favorite

00:00:38.182 --> 00:00:40.342
and I'd love to know which
one you guys liked the best.

00:00:40.342 --> 00:00:41.619
Also guys, as you're watching

00:00:41.619 --> 00:00:43.241
if you hear something that
really resonates with you,

00:00:43.241 --> 00:00:44.978
please leave it down in the comments below

00:00:44.978 --> 00:00:45.978
and put quotes around it

00:00:45.978 --> 00:00:47.746
so other people can be inspired.

00:00:47.746 --> 00:00:49.290
Also, when you write it down,

00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:51.787
it's much more likely to
lock in for yourself as well.

00:00:51.787 --> 00:00:52.620
Enjoy!

00:00:56.027 --> 00:00:58.777
(dramatic music)

00:01:05.796 --> 00:01:09.929
I realize that to have
the level of success

00:01:09.929 --> 00:01:11.719
that I want to have,

00:01:11.719 --> 00:01:13.877
it's difficult to spread it out

00:01:13.877 --> 00:01:16.321
and do multiple things,

00:01:16.321 --> 00:01:19.196
in order to be world class.

00:01:19.196 --> 00:01:22.829
And I made a decision, I
want to be world class.

00:01:22.829 --> 00:01:26.329
It takes such a desperate, obsessive focus

00:01:28.417 --> 00:01:32.817
to excel on a level that
I want to make movies.

00:01:32.817 --> 00:01:34.705
I was, Star Wars, when I was young,

00:01:34.705 --> 00:01:36.683
I sat in the movie theater
and watched Star Wars

00:01:36.683 --> 00:01:38.382
and I just couldn't believe

00:01:38.382 --> 00:01:40.417
that that movie made me feel like that,

00:01:40.417 --> 00:01:43.902
just floored and just
stunned by the creativity.

00:01:43.902 --> 00:01:45.467
And just, I'm realizing that

00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:48.243
in order to move people in that way,

00:01:48.243 --> 00:01:50.332
in order to touch people in that way,

00:01:50.332 --> 00:01:53.077
you really got to focus
with all of your fiber

00:01:53.077 --> 00:01:55.285
and all of your heart
and all your creativity.

00:01:55.285 --> 00:01:57.252
The concept of improving lives

00:01:57.252 --> 00:01:59.805
runs through the center of everything I do

00:01:59.805 --> 00:02:01.305
and then I realize

00:02:02.376 --> 00:02:04.936
that the way to improve lives

00:02:04.936 --> 00:02:07.785
is to continually improve yourself, right?

00:02:07.785 --> 00:02:09.529
So with that,

00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:12.862
every morning when I get out of the bed,

00:02:13.872 --> 00:02:16.569
I haven't fixed everything
in the world yet.

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:18.485
So there's always something to do.

00:02:18.485 --> 00:02:22.453
And in this film I read
an interesting quote

00:02:22.453 --> 00:02:25.786
from the Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha,

00:02:26.653 --> 00:02:27.653
he said that

00:02:28.929 --> 00:02:32.929
good people have to get
out of the bed every day

00:02:33.875 --> 00:02:37.208
and try to empty the ocean with a ladle.

00:02:38.837 --> 00:02:40.556
And I thought that was,

00:02:40.556 --> 00:02:43.038
I knew that was profound
and I paused for a second

00:02:43.038 --> 00:02:44.384
and I said all right,

00:02:44.384 --> 00:02:45.903
what the hell is a ladle, right?

00:02:45.903 --> 00:02:47.503
(audience laughing)

00:02:47.503 --> 00:02:48.336
Right?

00:02:48.336 --> 00:02:50.548
So that, I just I touched it on my iPad,

00:02:50.548 --> 00:02:52.110
is ladle, oh it's like a big spoon,

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:53.634
a big spoon, okay.

00:02:53.634 --> 00:02:56.031
It's like a soup spoon, yeah.

00:02:56.031 --> 00:02:57.846
A soup spoon, I was like why a soup spoon?

00:02:57.846 --> 00:03:02.059
So trying to empty the
ocean with a soup spoon.

00:03:02.059 --> 00:03:05.263
As the mentality of how
you wake up every day

00:03:05.263 --> 00:03:07.774
to try to do good in the world.

00:03:07.774 --> 00:03:11.857
So for me I'm really driven
by continually trying

00:03:15.072 --> 00:03:18.405
to elevate my mind and elevate my spirit

00:03:19.765 --> 00:03:21.719
and care for my body

00:03:21.719 --> 00:03:24.502
and to be able to love as many people

00:03:24.502 --> 00:03:28.635
as effectively as possible
with this mystery of life

00:03:28.635 --> 00:03:29.757
that I've been given.

00:03:29.757 --> 00:03:33.007
For me, I never did anything for money.

00:03:33.901 --> 00:03:35.984
It was never about money.

00:03:37.565 --> 00:03:40.596
My experience has been when
people do things for money

00:03:40.596 --> 00:03:42.106
you make bad choices.

00:03:42.106 --> 00:03:45.483
Find what you love and then
you'll learn how to make money

00:03:45.483 --> 00:03:47.150
doing what you love.

00:03:48.021 --> 00:03:50.240
When I changed careers,

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.136
I was never changing
to something for money,

00:03:53.136 --> 00:03:55.847
I was changing to something I loved more.

00:03:55.847 --> 00:03:59.244
And that, to me that's really the only way

00:03:59.244 --> 00:04:00.477
to keep the passion.

00:04:00.477 --> 00:04:01.653
If you have two choices,

00:04:01.653 --> 00:04:05.820
and one is playing the piano
and another one is bowling,

00:04:07.933 --> 00:04:10.076
and you can make more money bowling

00:04:10.076 --> 00:04:12.131
but you love playing the piano more,

00:04:12.131 --> 00:04:13.396
you got to play the piano.

00:04:13.396 --> 00:04:14.813
It's like you'll tear yourself apart

00:04:14.813 --> 00:04:18.824
if you're not doing the thing
that you love most in life.

00:04:18.824 --> 00:04:20.519
And you know what it is right now.

00:04:20.519 --> 00:04:22.824
Right now, there's something that you love

00:04:22.824 --> 00:04:24.200
more than everything.

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:26.322
Whatever it is that you love crazy,

00:04:26.322 --> 00:04:28.169
has to be the thing that
you dedicate your life.

00:04:28.169 --> 00:04:32.336
You have to be able to be
vulnerable in front of anybody.

00:04:34.479 --> 00:04:37.494
You have to be comfortable looking silly,

00:04:37.494 --> 00:04:39.906
you have to be comfortable
making mistakes,

00:04:39.906 --> 00:04:41.957
and you have to break
the thing inside of you

00:04:41.957 --> 00:04:44.673
that doesn't want people to see.

00:04:44.673 --> 00:04:47.106
Because as soon as you
allow people to see,

00:04:47.106 --> 00:04:49.562
all of a sudden you get access to things

00:04:49.562 --> 00:04:53.133
that you didn't realize you had access to.

00:04:53.133 --> 00:04:55.749
For example, like a thing I used to do,

00:04:55.749 --> 00:04:59.952
is when I was probably 18 or 19 years old,

00:04:59.952 --> 00:05:01.530
I got in touch with those blocks,

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.798
the camera hates emotional blocks.

00:05:04.798 --> 00:05:06.829
You put a camera in somebody's face

00:05:06.829 --> 00:05:09.511
and they're uncomfortable
about delivering emotion,

00:05:09.511 --> 00:05:11.270
it looks fake and you feel it.

00:05:11.270 --> 00:05:14.364
You'll immediately know it's not real.

00:05:14.364 --> 00:05:16.736
Especially, look at
the size of this screen

00:05:16.736 --> 00:05:21.239
and in a shot that whole
screen could be just your eyes.

00:05:21.239 --> 00:05:25.455
So it's like, you can't hide
discomfort, uncertainty.

00:05:25.455 --> 00:05:29.324
So you have to be able to get comfortable

00:05:29.324 --> 00:05:31.519
just being anything,

00:05:31.519 --> 00:05:34.565
anything that you have to be for the role,

00:05:34.565 --> 00:05:36.851
you have to be comfortable being it.

00:05:36.851 --> 00:05:40.846
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:05:40.846 --> 00:05:43.196
I got in probably two weeks

00:05:43.196 --> 00:05:45.863
into my preparation for the film

00:05:47.277 --> 00:05:49.760
and my father was diagnosed with cancer.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.084
So we just started talking

00:05:52.084 --> 00:05:54.417
about love, time, and death.

00:05:55.479 --> 00:05:58.192
And we, it was some of the most open

00:05:58.192 --> 00:06:02.025
and powerful conversations
that we'd ever had.

00:06:04.813 --> 00:06:07.804
And they gave him six weeks

00:06:07.804 --> 00:06:11.315
and he actually, he lived for four months.

00:06:11.315 --> 00:06:15.106
So about three months into the six weeks,

00:06:15.106 --> 00:06:16.764
I go to see him one day

00:06:16.764 --> 00:06:20.305
and he said, "Man, this is embarrassing."

00:06:20.305 --> 00:06:21.262
And I said, "What, Daddy?"

00:06:21.262 --> 00:06:22.678
And he said, "Man, you tell everybody

00:06:22.678 --> 00:06:25.135
"you would be dead in six weeks.

00:06:25.135 --> 00:06:28.678
"Three months later you
still hanging around."

00:06:28.678 --> 00:06:31.032
Laughing is the elixir.

00:06:31.032 --> 00:06:33.304
And that was another thing
that my father taught me.

00:06:33.304 --> 00:06:38.146
He was joking all the way up
'til the end like laughing.

00:06:38.146 --> 00:06:41.983
You have to be able to laugh at everything

00:06:41.983 --> 00:06:45.107
and for me the beautiful part

00:06:45.107 --> 00:06:46.774
is that's my natural

00:06:48.961 --> 00:06:50.514
color on the spectrum.

00:06:50.514 --> 00:06:53.209
I naturally go to comedy.

00:06:53.209 --> 00:06:55.164
And when I'm looking at something

00:06:55.164 --> 00:06:58.586
I'm always trying to
find why that's funny.

00:06:58.586 --> 00:07:03.179
And it's been really really
helpful in this experience.

00:07:03.179 --> 00:07:06.538
And even just this point in my life,

00:07:06.538 --> 00:07:10.111
keep, remember to laugh,
remember to laugh.

00:07:10.111 --> 00:07:12.942
And spend time with people
that make you laugh,

00:07:12.942 --> 00:07:14.596
that is hugely important.

00:07:14.596 --> 00:07:16.690
As a child my parents always told me

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:17.761
you could be whatever you want to be,

00:07:17.761 --> 00:07:20.844
you could do whatever you want to do,

00:07:21.678 --> 00:07:24.142
and that office, that position,

00:07:24.142 --> 00:07:26.903
the highest office on
the face of the earth,

00:07:26.903 --> 00:07:29.339
it was something I heard
my parents saying it,

00:07:29.339 --> 00:07:30.888
but I didn't totally believe it.

00:07:30.888 --> 00:07:31.969
Yet I went out in the world

00:07:31.969 --> 00:07:34.516
and I carried myself
and I held my head high

00:07:34.516 --> 00:07:36.556
and I stood there and I
looked people in their eyes

00:07:36.556 --> 00:07:39.284
and I talked to people
as if I was deserving

00:07:39.284 --> 00:07:41.750
of everything that this
planet has to offer.

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:45.917
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:07:47.303 --> 00:07:51.036
So I just, I really want to
say to children out there

00:07:51.036 --> 00:07:53.668
and to people who are watching,

00:07:53.668 --> 00:07:56.128
Confucius said one time,

00:07:56.128 --> 00:07:59.582
he who says he can and
he who says he can't

00:07:59.582 --> 00:08:01.962
are both usually right.

00:08:01.962 --> 00:08:04.556
So I want to stand here before you

00:08:04.556 --> 00:08:06.598
and as I hold this award,

00:08:06.598 --> 00:08:08.525
I want to give love to my wife

00:08:08.525 --> 00:08:10.118
and I want you to keep in your heart

00:08:10.118 --> 00:08:13.388
just know that you can, know that you can.

00:08:13.388 --> 00:08:16.305
And the greatest thing in my career

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:20.299
has been the constant commitment

00:08:20.299 --> 00:08:23.044
to putting something
in the world of value.

00:08:23.044 --> 00:08:23.877
- Mhmm.

00:08:23.877 --> 00:08:25.333
- Not putting something in the world

00:08:25.333 --> 00:08:26.608
that makes me look hot.

00:08:26.608 --> 00:08:27.441
- Mhmm.

00:08:27.441 --> 00:08:28.366
- You know?
- Yeah.

00:08:28.366 --> 00:08:31.219
- So I think as long
as you can stay focused

00:08:31.219 --> 00:08:33.660
on delivering your art
and delivering yourself

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:35.014
and delivering your energy

00:08:35.014 --> 00:08:36.857
and delivering your ideas

00:08:36.857 --> 00:08:39.795
for the world to be better--

00:08:39.795 --> 00:08:41.795
If there was one concept

00:08:43.674 --> 00:08:46.174
that I would suggest to people

00:08:47.644 --> 00:08:51.227
to take a daily
confrontation with is fear.

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:58.099
The problem with fear is that it lies.

00:08:58.099 --> 00:09:00.099
So fear tells you, "Hey.

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:03.869
"If you say that to that girl

00:09:03.869 --> 00:09:06.853
"she's going to know she has you.

00:09:06.853 --> 00:09:09.331
"And she'll never really
be attracted to you

00:09:09.331 --> 00:09:12.867
"if she knows how much
you attracted to her.

00:09:12.867 --> 00:09:14.190
"Don't say that, no.

00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:17.912
"How we get her is when
she walks by, ignore her."

00:09:17.912 --> 00:09:21.090
(audience laughing)

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:21.923
- Puppet on your shoulder.

00:09:21.923 --> 00:09:24.647
- Fear tells you dumb like that.

00:09:24.647 --> 00:09:28.159
(audience laughing)

00:09:28.159 --> 00:09:31.742
So for me the daily
confrontation with fear

00:09:33.946 --> 00:09:35.763
has become a real practice for me.

00:09:35.763 --> 00:09:38.096
Since about three years ago,

00:09:39.569 --> 00:09:41.736
I went skydiving in Dubai.

00:09:42.622 --> 00:09:44.030
And skydiving,

00:09:44.030 --> 00:09:48.936
skydiving is a really
interesting confront with fear.

00:09:48.936 --> 00:09:50.969
So I got to stand up, I got to stand up.

00:09:50.969 --> 00:09:51.802
(interviewer laughing)

00:09:51.802 --> 00:09:53.002
I got to stand up.

00:09:53.002 --> 00:09:54.886
So all your friends,

00:09:54.886 --> 00:09:55.801
what happens you go out--

00:09:55.801 --> 00:09:57.206
- Hold this.
- How you, oh sorry.

00:09:57.206 --> 00:09:58.039
Oh, I dropped my thing.

00:09:58.039 --> 00:10:01.007
So what happens is you
go out the night before

00:10:01.007 --> 00:10:03.539
and you take a drink with your friends

00:10:03.539 --> 00:10:07.431
and somebody says, "Yeah, we
should go skydiving tomorrow!"

00:10:07.431 --> 00:10:09.020
And you go, "Yeah, we'll go skydiving!"

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:10.272
And we're "Yeah! Yeah!"

00:10:10.272 --> 00:10:11.306
And you're, "Yeah!"

00:10:11.306 --> 00:10:13.340
And everybody goes, "Yeah!"

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:15.163
And you go home, you're by yourself,

00:10:15.163 --> 00:10:16.080
you're like, "Mm."

00:10:16.080 --> 00:10:18.493
(audience laughing)

00:10:18.493 --> 00:10:21.601
You're like, "Well yeah, I
mean they was drunk too."

00:10:21.601 --> 00:10:23.684
(audience laughing)

00:10:23.684 --> 00:10:25.338
So maybe they not,

00:10:25.338 --> 00:10:27.601
maybe, I mean we don't have to go,

00:10:27.601 --> 00:10:29.184
we don't have to do it.

00:10:29.184 --> 00:10:30.974
So then that night
you're laying in your bed

00:10:30.974 --> 00:10:32.474
and you just keep,

00:10:33.724 --> 00:10:34.557
and you're terrified.

00:10:34.557 --> 00:10:36.980
You keep imagining over and over again

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:38.632
jumping out of an airplane.

00:10:38.632 --> 00:10:41.709
And you can't figure out
why you would do that.

00:10:41.709 --> 00:10:42.550
And you're laying there

00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:44.576
and you have the worst
night's sleep of your life

00:10:44.576 --> 00:10:48.308
but you still have the hope
that your friends were drunk.

00:10:48.308 --> 00:10:49.932
So you wake up the next day

00:10:49.932 --> 00:10:51.490
and you go down where you say

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:53.913
where you were going to
meet and everybody's there.

00:10:53.913 --> 00:10:55.413
You're like oh sh.

00:10:56.865 --> 00:10:57.698
Alright, alright.

00:10:57.698 --> 00:10:59.985
Cool cool cool cool cool cool.

00:10:59.985 --> 00:11:01.287
So you get in the van

00:11:01.287 --> 00:11:02.689
and you don't know

00:11:02.689 --> 00:11:05.197
that your friends had the
same night that you had

00:11:05.197 --> 00:11:06.777
'cause they're pretended like they didn't.

00:11:06.777 --> 00:11:09.047
They're like, "Yeah man,
my uncle's a Navy SEAL

00:11:09.047 --> 00:11:11.035
"and this is going to be great.

00:11:11.035 --> 00:11:12.010
"I've been looking forward to this."

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:14.696
And you're like, "Oh my god, oh my god."

00:11:14.696 --> 00:11:16.065
And your stomach is terrible.

00:11:16.065 --> 00:11:17.436
You can't eat and everything

00:11:17.436 --> 00:11:19.354
but you don't want to be the only punk

00:11:19.354 --> 00:11:21.505
who doesn't jump out of this airplane.

00:11:21.505 --> 00:11:25.226
So you get there and then
you have the safety brief

00:11:25.226 --> 00:11:26.226
and you're standing there

00:11:26.226 --> 00:11:27.059
and the guys will tell you,

00:11:27.059 --> 00:11:28.071
"Well if the chute doesn't open

00:11:28.071 --> 00:11:29.678
"what's going to happen is you do a--"

00:11:29.678 --> 00:11:31.389
Well why the hell, why, what could happen?

00:11:31.389 --> 00:11:33.792
That the shoot wouldn't open?

00:11:33.792 --> 00:11:36.850
So you do a thing and what
you do is your first jump,

00:11:36.850 --> 00:11:38.366
you're attached to a guy

00:11:38.366 --> 00:11:40.803
who is going, he's going to walk you out.

00:11:40.803 --> 00:11:42.867
So you go and you get there

00:11:42.867 --> 00:11:45.799
and there's an airplane
and nobody's stopping.

00:11:45.799 --> 00:11:47.250
Everybody's still going.

00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:49.255
So you get onto the airplane

00:11:49.255 --> 00:11:51.955
and you're sitting there and it's extra

00:11:51.955 --> 00:11:53.885
'cause you're sitting on some
dude's lap, some stranger.

00:11:53.885 --> 00:11:55.519
(audience and interviewer laughing)

00:11:55.519 --> 00:11:56.793
You're sitting on his lap

00:11:56.793 --> 00:11:58.299
and it's all, you're
trying to make small talk.

00:11:58.299 --> 00:11:59.132
"Yeah. man.

00:11:59.132 --> 00:12:02.340
(audience and interviewer laughing)

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:07.207
"So you'd be jumping with
people all the time, huh?"

00:12:07.207 --> 00:12:09.323
So and then you just want to make sure,

00:12:09.323 --> 00:12:10.231
"You got kids, right?

00:12:10.231 --> 00:12:12.709
"You got people you need to see?"

00:12:12.709 --> 00:12:14.197
Just want to make sure he's serious.

00:12:14.197 --> 00:12:15.096
So you get in there.

00:12:15.096 --> 00:12:16.212
So everything's normal.

00:12:16.212 --> 00:12:17.650
So you fly and you go up,

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:18.724
you go up, you go up,

00:12:18.724 --> 00:12:20.257
and you go up to 14,000 feet

00:12:20.257 --> 00:12:22.724
and you notice there's a light,

00:12:22.724 --> 00:12:26.329
it's red and it's yellow and green.

00:12:26.329 --> 00:12:27.921
So right now the light's red,

00:12:27.921 --> 00:12:29.087
so then you start thinking

00:12:29.087 --> 00:12:30.563
at some point the
light's going to go green

00:12:30.563 --> 00:12:32.939
but you don't know what's going to happen.

00:12:32.939 --> 00:12:34.775
And you wait and it goes yellow

00:12:34.775 --> 00:12:36.314
and the light goes green

00:12:36.314 --> 00:12:37.988
and somebody opens the door

00:12:37.988 --> 00:12:39.551
and in that moment you realize

00:12:39.551 --> 00:12:42.594
you've never been in a freaking
airplane with the door open.

00:12:42.594 --> 00:12:46.269
(audience laughing)

00:12:46.269 --> 00:12:48.976
Terror, oh sorry I'm spitting.

00:12:48.976 --> 00:12:50.132
(imitating loud spitting)

00:12:50.132 --> 00:12:50.965
Oh sorry.

00:12:50.965 --> 00:12:53.298
Terror terror terror terror!

00:12:55.146 --> 00:12:58.229
So you go and then if you were smart,

00:12:59.593 --> 00:13:02.503
you sat in the back so you don't go first.

00:13:02.503 --> 00:13:05.256
And then people start
going out of the airplane.

00:13:05.256 --> 00:13:09.423
And you go and the guy walks
you up to the end of the thing

00:13:10.604 --> 00:13:13.857
and you're standing and
your toes are on the edge.

00:13:13.857 --> 00:13:16.940
And you're looking out down to death.

00:13:19.709 --> 00:13:21.887
And they say, "On three."

00:13:21.887 --> 00:13:24.063
And they say, "One, two!"

00:13:24.063 --> 00:13:25.410
And he pushes you on two

00:13:25.410 --> 00:13:27.175
because people grab on three.

00:13:27.175 --> 00:13:31.098
(audience and interviewer laughing)

00:13:31.098 --> 00:13:32.206
And you go--

00:13:32.206 --> 00:13:35.130
(imitating screaming)

00:13:35.130 --> 00:13:37.242
And you fall out of the airplane

00:13:37.242 --> 00:13:39.659
and in one second you realize

00:13:40.764 --> 00:13:44.931
that it's the most blissful
experience of your life.

00:13:45.775 --> 00:13:47.598
You're flying.

00:13:47.598 --> 00:13:49.580
It doesn't feel like falling.

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:51.209
It's like that you're actually are kind of

00:13:51.209 --> 00:13:53.314
held a little bit by the wind

00:13:53.314 --> 00:13:54.640
and then you start

00:13:54.640 --> 00:13:56.428
and you start falling, you're falling,

00:13:56.428 --> 00:13:58.703
and you, there's zero fear.

00:13:58.703 --> 00:14:02.208
You realize that the
point of maximum danger

00:14:02.208 --> 00:14:05.273
is the point of minimum fear.

00:14:05.273 --> 00:14:06.972
It's bliss!

00:14:06.972 --> 00:14:08.232
It's bliss!

00:14:08.232 --> 00:14:09.732
And you're flying.

00:14:12.604 --> 00:14:13.771
And you're doing it

00:14:13.771 --> 00:14:17.951
and then 20 seconds,
25 seconds, 40 seconds.

00:14:17.951 --> 00:14:20.209
And you have enough time
to just kind of be like,

00:14:20.209 --> 00:14:24.600
ah, there's that building
I saw that morning.

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:27.335
Oh you can see the ocean!

00:14:27.335 --> 00:14:28.635
You start doing all of that

00:14:28.635 --> 00:14:31.158
and the lesson for me was

00:14:31.158 --> 00:14:34.493
why were you scared in
your bed the night before?

00:14:34.493 --> 00:14:37.569
Why did you, what do
you need that fear for?

00:14:37.569 --> 00:14:40.408
Just don't go, why are
you scared in your bed

00:14:40.408 --> 00:14:43.090
16 hours before you jump?

00:14:43.090 --> 00:14:44.924
Why are you scared in the car?

00:14:44.924 --> 00:14:47.757
Why could you not enjoy breakfast?

00:14:48.997 --> 00:14:51.831
What did you need that, the fear is,

00:14:51.831 --> 00:14:53.314
fear of what?

00:14:53.314 --> 00:14:55.210
You're nowhere even near the airplane.

00:14:55.210 --> 00:14:58.043
Everything up to the stepping out,

00:14:59.107 --> 00:15:02.007
there's actually no reason to be scared

00:15:02.007 --> 00:15:04.640
it only just ruins your day.

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:08.160
You don't have to jump
and then in that moment,

00:15:08.160 --> 00:15:10.663
all of a sudden where
you should be terrified

00:15:10.663 --> 00:15:13.890
is the most blissful
experience of your life.

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:17.738
And God placed the best things in life

00:15:17.738 --> 00:15:20.059
on the other side of terror,

00:15:20.059 --> 00:15:22.313
on the other side of your maximum fear

00:15:22.313 --> 00:15:24.452
are all of the best things in life.

00:15:24.452 --> 00:15:27.994
I have to show up at work
with the right attitude

00:15:27.994 --> 00:15:31.213
and with the positive energy.

00:15:31.213 --> 00:15:34.796
And I actually had a
t-shirt made that said

00:15:35.684 --> 00:15:39.851
"Positive energy is a part
of your job description."

00:15:41.065 --> 00:15:45.388
So the idea that I want to
show up with the right energy,

00:15:45.388 --> 00:15:47.515
I want to show up, we're all here.

00:15:47.515 --> 00:15:49.311
Even now we're all here,

00:15:49.311 --> 00:15:51.441
we're trying to feed our families,

00:15:51.441 --> 00:15:54.671
we're trying to have a good time.

00:15:54.671 --> 00:15:57.436
This is an important part of our lives,

00:15:57.436 --> 00:15:59.677
our time is all that we have.

00:15:59.677 --> 00:16:01.919
So to me it's hugely important

00:16:01.919 --> 00:16:05.299
to deliver positive energy in a way

00:16:05.299 --> 00:16:07.427
because it's viral.

00:16:07.427 --> 00:16:10.251
So if I come with positive energy,

00:16:10.251 --> 00:16:12.889
then someone else is going to
pick up on the positive energy.

00:16:12.889 --> 00:16:15.006
And you're going to take
it home to your families.

00:16:15.006 --> 00:16:17.610
So for me it's just hugely important

00:16:17.610 --> 00:16:21.332
to approach everything and
everybody at every turn

00:16:21.332 --> 00:16:24.499
with the most positive loving kindness

00:16:25.605 --> 00:16:27.447
that I can generate.

00:16:27.447 --> 00:16:28.795
